Read it. Here's the problem and the fix.

## The performance problem

```cpp
std::string out;
for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;  // repeated reallocation
return out;
```

Each `out += p` appends to a string whose buffer may be too small, forcing a **reallocation**: allocate a bigger buffer, copy all existing characters over, free the old one. With many/large parts this is O(n²) total work — every append can trigger a full copy of everything accumulated so far.

## The idiomatic fix

When the total size is known (it is — it's just the sum of the part lengths), **reserve once** before the loop:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::size_t total = 0;
  for (const auto& p : parts) total += p.size();

  std::string out;
  out.reserve(total);          // one allocation, exactly the right size
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;   // no reallocation now
  return out;
}
```

`reserve(total)` makes a single allocation of the exact final size, so every subsequent `+=` is a plain append with **zero reallocations** — total work drops to O(n).

